<p><em>Study text for 14 CFR Part 107 Remote Pilot applicants</em></p>
<p>2026 EDITION  Built from the course presentation and updated against current FAA Part 107 guidance. Where older slide language conflicts with current FAA rules, the current rule is identified in the text.</p>
<h2 id="how-to-use-this-chapter"><strong>How to Use This Chapter</strong></h2>
<p>The FAA knowledge test does not reward memorizing isolated definitions. Many questions present an operating situation and ask what the remote pilot in command should do. As you study, focus on three things: who is responsible, what operating limitation applies, and what action keeps the operation compliant and safe.</p>
<ul>
<li>Learn the bold terms and numerical limits.</li>
<li>Work through each scenario before reading the answer.</li>
<li>Pay special attention to Exam Tip and Key Number boxes.</li>
<li>Use the end-of-chapter questions without notes, then review weak areas.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="learning-objectives"><strong>Learning Objectives</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li>Explain when Part 107 applies to a small UAS operation.</li>
<li>Describe Remote Pilot Certificate eligibility and recency requirements.</li>
<li>Explain the authority and responsibilities of the remote PIC.</li>
<li>Identify crew roles and limitations.</li>
<li>Apply registration, documentation, inspection, accident-reporting, and preflight rules.</li>
<li>Apply VLOS, right-of-way, speed, altitude, visibility, cloud-clearance, night, and moving-vehicle rules.</li>
<li>Recognize requirements for operations over people and Remote ID.</li>
<li>Explain the purpose of LAANC and Part 107 waivers.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="1-1-the-part-107-regulatory-framework"><strong>1.1 The Part 107 Regulatory Framework</strong></h2>
<p>Small unmanned aircraft operations in the United States are governed primarily by Title 14 of the Code of Federal Regulations, Part 107. Part 107 establishes operating rules, pilot certification requirements, and limitations for civil small UAS operations.</p>
<p>A small unmanned aircraft is an aircraft operated without the possibility of direct human intervention from within or on the aircraft. Under Part 107, a small UAS must weigh less than 55 pounds at takeoff, including everything onboard or attached to the aircraft. Recreational operations may instead be conducted under the statutory recreational exception when all of its conditions are met; Part 107 is the normal framework for non-recreational work, business, research, education, and other civil operations that do not qualify for that exception.</p>
<p>The remote pilot should think of Part 107 as the baseline rule set. Some operations are permitted directly under the rule, some require airspace authorization, and some require an FAA waiver.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">KEY NUMBER  55 lb is the upper weight boundary for a small unmanned aircraft operating under Part 107. The aircraft must be less than 55 lb at takeoff, including payload and attachments.</p>
<h2 id="1-2-becoming-a-certificated-remote-pilot"><strong>1.2 Becoming a Certificated Remote Pilot</strong></h2>
<p>A person acting as remote PIC under Part 107 must hold an FAA Remote Pilot Certificate with a small UAS rating. For a first-time applicant, the FAA currently requires the applicant to be at least 16 years old, be able to read, speak, write, and understand English, be in a physical and mental condition that allows safe operation, and pass the initial Unmanned Aircraft General – Small (UAG) aeronautical knowledge test.</p>
<p>Applicants who already hold an eligible Part 61 pilot certificate and have a current flight review have an alternate training path. The course slides also identify the FSDO, designated pilot examiner, airman certification representative, and certificated flight instructor as persons or offices that can process appropriate applications.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">EXAM TIP  The Remote Pilot Certificate itself does not expire. To exercise Part 107 privileges, however, the pilot must maintain aeronautical knowledge recency. Current FAA recurrent training is completed online within the preceding 24 calendar months.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">KEY NUMBER  24 calendar months: recurrent aeronautical knowledge requirement.</p>
<h2 id="1-3-remote-pic-authority-and-responsibility"><strong>1.3 Remote PIC Authority and Responsibility</strong></h2>
<p>Every Part 107 operation must have a remote pilot in command. The remote PIC is directly responsible for the operation and is the final authority as to the operation of the small UAS.</p>
<p>The remote PIC must be designated before or during the flight and must ensure that the operation complies with applicable regulations. The PIC must also ensure that the operation poses no undue hazard to people, aircraft, or property if control of the aircraft is lost.</p>
<p>This authority carries a practical consequence: automation does not transfer responsibility to the aircraft. Even when a mission is flown using an autopilot, the remote PIC remains responsible for the flight and must be able to respond to an unsafe condition.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">SCENARIO  A mapping aircraft is flying an automated grid when a helicopter appears near the operating area. The autopilot is functioning normally. The remote PIC is still responsible for avoiding the helicopter and must take whatever action is necessary to yield right-of-way.</p>
<h2 id="1-4-supporting-crew-roles"><strong>1.4 Supporting Crew Roles</strong></h2>
<p>A person manipulating the flight controls does not necessarily have to hold a Remote Pilot Certificate. A non-certificated person may manipulate the controls when directly supervised by a certificated remote PIC who can immediately take direct control of the aircraft.</p>
<p>A visual observer (VO) is an optional crewmember under ordinary Part 107 operations. The VO helps maintain awareness of the aircraft, other aircraft, people, obstacles, and other hazards. When a VO is used, the remote PIC, person manipulating the controls, and VO must maintain effective communication.</p>
<p>A remote PIC or visual observer may not serve in that role for more than one small UAS operation at the same time.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">EXAM TIP  Do not confuse the person touching the controls with the remote PIC. The remote PIC is the person who carries regulatory responsibility for the operation.</p>
<h2 id="1-5-registration-and-required-documents"><strong>1.5 Registration and Required Documents</strong></h2>
<p>Part 107 aircraft must be registered with the FAA and marked as required. The remote pilot should verify registration status before flight and ensure that required information is available for inspection.</p>
<p>The course presentation emphasizes that required documentation may be physical or digital. The remote pilot should have access to the Remote Pilot Certificate, aircraft registration, applicable waivers or exemptions, and other documents required for the operation.</p>
<p>The FAA may request inspection or testing of the small UAS. A professional operator should therefore treat documentation as part of preflight readiness rather than as an administrative afterthought.</p>
<h2 id="1-6-preflight-action-and-airworthiness"><strong>1.6 Preflight Action and Airworthiness</strong></h2>
<p>Before flight, the remote PIC must become familiar with information relevant to the operation. This includes the local operating environment, weather, airspace, people and property on the surface, obstacles, and other factors that could affect safety.</p>
<p>The remote PIC must ensure that the small UAS is in a condition for safe operation. A practical preflight includes the airframe, propellers, motors, batteries, control links, navigation system, payload attachment, firmware or mission configuration, and any equipment required for the intended operation.</p>
<p>Part 107 places responsibility on the remote PIC to determine whether the aircraft is safe to fly. If a condition arises that makes continued flight unsafe, the operation should be discontinued.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">SCENARIO  During preflight you discover a propeller with a visible crack near the hub. Even if the aircraft powers up normally, it is not in a condition for safe operation. Replace the damaged component before flight.</p>
<h2 id="1-7-accident-reporting"><strong>1.7 Accident Reporting</strong></h2>
<p>Certain Part 107 accidents must be reported to the FAA. The course material emphasizes two reportable thresholds: serious injury to any person or any loss of consciousness, and damage to property other than the small unmanned aircraft when the repair or replacement cost exceeds the regulatory threshold.</p>
<p>The report must be made within 10 days of the operation. Students should distinguish FAA reporting requirements from any additional reporting that may be required by an employer, insurer, property owner, law-enforcement agency, or the NTSB.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">KEY NUMBER  10 days: time allowed to submit a required Part 107 accident report to the FAA. $500: property-damage threshold identified in the course material, excluding damage to the sUAS itself.</p>
<h2 id="1-8-alcohol-drugs-and-fitness-for-flight"><strong>1.8 Alcohol, Drugs, and Fitness for Flight</strong></h2>
<p>Part 107 prohibits a remote PIC, person manipulating the controls, visual observer, or other crewmember from participating when impaired by alcohol or drugs. The course presentation uses the same key alcohol limits familiar to manned aviation: no alcoholic beverage within the preceding 8 hours, no operation while under the influence, and no operation with a blood alcohol concentration of 0.04 percent or greater.</p>
<p>A crewmember also may not participate while using a drug that affects mental or physical capabilities in a way that makes the operation unsafe.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">KEY NUMBER  8 hours bottle-to-throttle; 0.04% blood alcohol concentration.</p>
<h2 id="1-9-visual-line-of-sight"><strong>1.9 Visual Line of Sight</strong></h2>
<p>Visual line of sight (VLOS) is a central Part 107 operating requirement. The aircraft must remain close enough to the remote PIC or visual observer for the aircraft&#x27;s location, attitude, altitude, direction of flight, and hazards to be determined with unaided vision.</p>
<p>Corrective lenses are permitted because they are considered normal vision correction. Binoculars and similar devices may be used momentarily, but they are not a substitute for the required unaided visual capability.</p>
<p>First-person-view video can be a useful piloting aid, but a camera image by itself does not satisfy the VLOS requirement.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">EXAM TIP  The important concept is not simply &#x27;I can see a dot.&#x27; The crew must be able to know the aircraft&#x27;s position and movement and scan for hazards.</p>
<h2 id="1-10-right-of-way-and-operating-near-other-aircraft"><strong>1.10 Right-of-Way and Operating Near Other Aircraft</strong></h2>
<p>Small UAS must yield the right-of-way to all other aircraft. The remote PIC may not pass over, under, or ahead of another aircraft unless well clear. Because a small drone can be difficult for a manned-aircraft pilot to see, the UAS crew should never assume that another pilot will maneuver around the drone.</p>
<p>Operations near airports require particular care. A remote PIC may not interfere with airport operations or traffic patterns. Controlled airspace may require FAA authorization, but authorization does not eliminate the requirement to see and avoid other aircraft.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">SCENARIO  You have a valid LAANC authorization and are flying at 150 ft AGL when a medical helicopter approaches below normal traffic-pattern altitude. The authorization does not give the drone priority. Yield immediately and remain well clear.</p>
<h2 id="1-11-core-operating-limitations"><strong>1.11 Core Operating Limitations</strong></h2>
<p>Several numerical limits appear repeatedly on the FAA knowledge test. Learn them as a group:</p>
