<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Limitation</th>
<th scope="col">Standard Part 107 value</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Maximum groundspeed</th>
<td>100 mph (87 knots)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Maximum altitude</th>
<td>400 ft AGL, subject to the structure provision</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Minimum flight visibility</th>
<td>3 statute miles</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Cloud clearance below clouds</th>
<td>500 ft</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Horizontal cloud clearance</th>
<td>2,000 ft</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Part 107 also contains a structure provision that can permit operation higher than 400 ft AGL when the aircraft remains within the allowed distance of a structure and does not exceed the permitted height above the structure&#x27;s uppermost limit. Airspace restrictions still apply.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">EXAM TIP  When a test question gives several numbers, first identify whether it is asking about speed, altitude, visibility, or cloud clearance. Do not mix the 500-ft vertical cloud clearance with the 400-ft normal AGL ceiling.</p>
<h2 id="1-12-night-operations"><strong>1.12 Night Operations</strong></h2>
<p>Older Part 107 training materials stated that routine night operations required a waiver. That is no longer the general rule. Current Part 107 rules permit routine night operations when the remote PIC has completed the applicable updated knowledge test or recurrent training and the aircraft has qualifying anti-collision lighting.</p>
<p>The anti-collision lighting must be visible for at least 3 statute miles and have a flash rate sufficient to avoid a collision. The remote PIC may reduce the intensity when operating conditions make that action safer, but the lighting requirement remains part of the night-operation rule.</p>
<p>Night operations in controlled airspace still require the appropriate airspace authorization.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">KEY NUMBER  3 statute miles: required visibility distance for the aircraft&#x27;s anti-collision lighting during Part 107 night operations.</p>
<h2 id="1-13-operations-over-people-and-moving-vehicles"><strong>1.13 Operations Over People and Moving Vehicles</strong></h2>
<p>Part 107 now provides four categories that can allow qualifying small UAS operations over people. The categories are based on aircraft characteristics, injury risk, operating restrictions, and—in some cases—FAA declarations of compliance or airworthiness certification.</p>
<p>Category 1 applies to very small aircraft weighing 0.55 lb or less, including everything attached or carried, and the aircraft may not have exposed rotating parts that would cause lacerations. Categories 2 and 3 use performance-based requirements and require aircraft eligibility and an FAA-accepted declaration of compliance. Category 3 has more restrictive operating conditions. Category 4 applies to eligible aircraft with an airworthiness certificate and associated operating limitations.</p>
<p>Operations over moving vehicles are also allowed only when the applicable regulatory conditions are satisfied. A student should avoid the oversimplified rule that a drone can &#x27;never&#x27; fly over a vehicle; the current regulation is conditional.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">EXAM TIP  For operations-over-people questions, identify the aircraft category first. Then ask whether the people are participants, whether the site is restricted-access, whether flight is sustained, and whether Remote ID requirements apply.</p>
<h2 id="1-14-remote-identification"><strong>1.14 Remote Identification</strong></h2>
<p>Remote Identification (Remote ID) provides identification and location information about drones in flight. In general, drones that are required to be registered must comply with the Remote ID rule unless an exception applies.</p>
<p>A Standard Remote ID drone has compliant Remote ID capability built into the aircraft. An operator may also use an FAA-compliant broadcast module on an eligible aircraft. The course presentation emphasizes three practical responsibilities: the equipment must be functioning, it may not be intentionally disabled or tampered with, and if required Remote ID broadcasting fails, the pilot must land as soon as practicable.</p>
<p>Another compliance path is operation within an FAA-Recognized Identification Area (FRIA), subject to the conditions that apply to that type of operation.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">EXAM TIP  Remote ID is an aircraft-operation requirement, not a substitute for Part 107 certification, registration, airspace authorization, or VLOS.</p>
<h2 id="1-15-laanc-and-controlled-airspace-authorization"><strong>1.15 LAANC and Controlled Airspace Authorization</strong></h2>
<p>The Low Altitude Authorization and Notification Capability (LAANC) is an FAA system that allows eligible drone pilots to request access to controlled airspace at low altitude through FAA-approved service suppliers. It provides pilots with information about where operations may be authorized and gives air traffic personnel visibility into planned drone activity.</p>
<p>LAANC is an authorization mechanism; it is not permission to disregard other Part 107 rules. The pilot remains responsible for weather, VLOS, right-of-way, operating limitations, local conditions, and any terms contained in the authorization.</p>
<h2 id="1-16-waivers"><strong>1.16 Waivers</strong></h2>
<p>A Part 107 waiver allows the FAA to authorize certain operations that would otherwise not comply with a waivable provision of Part 107. The applicant must demonstrate that the proposed operation can be conducted safely under the terms of the waiver.</p>
<p>Not every Part 107 provision is waivable. Common waiver areas include certain VLOS, multiple-aircraft, operating-limitations, moving-vehicle, and operations-over-people provisions. Modern Part 107 rules mean that routine night operations meeting the standard night requirements generally do not need a waiver.</p>
<p>A waiver is not a blanket exemption from Part 107. The operator must comply with the waiver&#x27;s specific conditions and all other regulations that remain applicable.</p>
<h2 id="1-17-chapter-summary"><strong>1.17 Chapter Summary</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li>Part 107 is the primary FAA rule set for civil small UAS operations that do not qualify for another operating framework.</li>
<li>The remote PIC is the final authority and carries responsibility for regulatory compliance and operational safety.</li>
<li>A non-certificated person may manipulate the controls under the direct supervision of a qualified remote PIC who can immediately take control.</li>
<li>Aircraft registration, required documents, preflight inspection, and safe-condition determinations are operational responsibilities.</li>
<li>Report qualifying accidents to the FAA within 10 days.</li>
<li>Remember the core limits: 100 mph/87 kt, 400 ft AGL, 3 SM visibility, 500 ft below clouds, and 2,000 ft horizontally from clouds.</li>
<li>Small UAS always yield right-of-way to other aircraft.</li>
<li>Routine night operations are permitted when current training/knowledge and anti-collision-light requirements are met.</li>
<li>Operations over people and moving vehicles depend on specific regulatory categories and conditions.</li>
<li>Remote ID and airspace authorization are separate compliance requirements.</li>
<li>LAANC can provide controlled-airspace authorization; a Part 107 waiver can permit certain otherwise prohibited operations when the FAA approves an equivalent level of safety.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="1-18-knowledge-check"><strong>1.18 Knowledge Check</strong></h2>
<ol>
<li>What is the maximum takeoff weight for an aircraft to qualify as a small UAS under Part 107?</li>
<li>Does a Remote Pilot Certificate expire? What must a pilot do to maintain aeronautical knowledge recency?</li>
<li>Who is the final authority for a Part 107 operation?</li>
<li>May a person without a Remote Pilot Certificate manipulate the controls during a Part 107 flight?</li>
<li>Is a visual observer required for every Part 107 operation?</li>
<li>Within how many days must a qualifying Part 107 accident be reported to the FAA?</li>
<li>What is the standard maximum Part 107 groundspeed?</li>
<li>What is the standard maximum altitude above ground level?</li>
<li>What is the minimum flight visibility?</li>
<li>What are the vertical and horizontal cloud-clearance requirements?</li>
<li>Which aircraft has right-of-way in an encounter between a small UAS and a manned aircraft?</li>
<li>What two primary requirements allow routine Part 107 night operations?</li>
<li>What is the maximum weight of a Category 1 aircraft used for operations over people?</li>
<li>What should a pilot do if required Remote ID broadcasting fails during flight?</li>
<li>What does LAANC provide?</li>
<li>Does a LAANC authorization give the drone priority over other aircraft?</li>
<li>What is the alcohol &#x27;bottle-to-throttle&#x27; period emphasized in the course?</li>
<li>What blood alcohol concentration is prohibited?</li>
<li>Can FPV video alone satisfy the Part 107 VLOS requirement?</li>
<li>What is the purpose of a Part 107 waiver?</li>
</ol>
